+WHAT HE WAS RESPONDING TO, SECOND QUOTE. The same thing, said more generally, about the volume across
+the whole session — status reports carrying round counts, hash rebinds, gate mechanics and process
+narration alongside the one or two facts that mattered.
+
+HIS WORDS, verbatim: "you all constantly flooding the zone with shot"
+
+WHAT IT MEANS IN PRACTICE. Give him the decision and its consequence, in two sentences. Process
+detail — review rounds, gate mechanics, hash rebinding, stash dances, contract amendments — goes in
+the repo, where it belongs, and never into a message to him. If a paragraph explains HOW the work was
+governed rather than WHAT is true or WHAT he must decide, delete it before sending.
+⚠ A useful test before sending: could he act on this, or is it me showing my work? He does not need
+to know that a gate blocked, only what is now true.
+
+⚠ WHAT IT DOES NOT LICENSE. It is not permission to stop recording any of it — the contract,
+acceptance evidence, review and this log all stay exactly as they are, because that is what makes the
+work checkable. It changes what goes in a CHAT MESSAGE, not what goes in the repo. It is also not
+licence to hide a problem: a defect, a red test or a blocked decision still gets said, in one
+sentence, immediately.

